    "q-331906": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331906",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-4{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=5{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-4{x}_{1}(t)-5{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-5L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=5L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1=-4{X}_{1}(s)-5{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2=5{X}_{1}(s)-3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-331906-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
