    "q-331918": {
      "dom_id": "step-q331918",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3=-2{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+4=-3{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get \n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-3=-2{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s)$ gives\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+4=-3(\\frac{3}{s+2})+4{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-4s-17}{(s-4)(s+2)}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
